description:
Motivation: end-to-end latency in mixed prefill+decode workloads is dominated by idle gaps between prefill stages and the decode stage competing for the same GPU kernels. This PR introduces a stage-parallel execution model plus a speculative chunked prefill path to reduce tail latency without regressing throughput or memory usage. Key ideas: 1) Chunked prefill: break long prefill sequences into configurable chunks (default 128 tokens) so earlier chunks can be warmed into the KV cache and start decode sooner. 2) Speculative prefill: when downstream decode capacity is idle, the scheduler will issue prefill chunks for likely-following tokens (learned via simple n-gram hinting / recent traffic distribution) up to a configurable speculative_depth. Speculation is conservative and reversible. 3) Stage overlap: overlap kernel launches for later prefill chunks with the first decode iterations where memory permits (KV prefixes are streamed to the decode workload). 4) Scheduler: new stage_parallel scheduler coordinates bandwidth (SM time) tokens and lockless KV updates, prioritizing decode-critical work when latency SLOs are at risk and pulling speculative prefill only when there is headroom. 5) Kernel surface: add partial-prefill kernels and a KV-append fast-path to avoid full KV re-materialization when appending chunks. 6) Continuous batching adjustments: allow prefill chunk boundaries to be coalesced across requests while preserving per-request token ordering. Implementation summary: - runtime/prefill_scheduler.rs: new scheduler implementation, configuration flags, and a lightweight token accounting allocator. - runtime/kernels/partial_prefill.cu and .rs bindings: new kernels for chunked attention state updates and incremental KV writes. - runtime/kv_cache/streaming.rs: streaming append API, lockless CAS helpers, and metrics. - runtime/batching/continuous.rs: changes to allow chunk coalescing and backpressure signals to the scheduler. - middleware/router: new header X-Redwood-Prefill-Chunk to enable client experiments. Compatibility and safety: speculative prefill is behind feature flag "speculative_prefill" and limited by both memory budget and speculative_depth (default 1). If a misprediction is detected the scheduler discards speculative KV entries without touching the authoritative cache used by other in-flight requests. Tests & validation: - Microbenchmarks: internal tester/latency_bench harness showed median latency improvement of 12% and P99 improvement of 18-24% on a mix of 2k prefill + 256 decode token workloads with batch sizes 1-4. - Throughput: end-to-end throughput unchanged or +~12% in mixed workloads due to better kernel utilization. - Regression tests: expanded kv_cache unit tests, added integration test that replays real production traces with configurable speculation seeds. Rollout plan: gated by feature flag and staged canary (5% -> 25% -> 100%) for both hosted and dedicated. We include an opt-out header so customers can pin behavior per-route. Checklist: - [x] Chunking API and defaults implemented - [x] Speculation controller with conservative defaults - [x] Kernels added and profiled for memory and latency - [x] Unit and integration tests - [x] CI green and canary runbook written - [x] Docs: runtime tuning section updated Breaking considerations: no breaking API changes; new behaviors are opt-in and controlled by flags and headers. Observability: new metrics pref_chunk_ops, pref_speculative_hits, pref_speculative_discards, stage_overlap_utilization, avg_kv_append_lat_ms. Suggested follow-ups: smarter speculation model (learned), improved chunk sizing heuristic (size per request distribution), kernel fusion for partial-prefill + decode merge. Code pointers and short snippet (illustrative): "// scheduler main loop
loop {
  budget = allocator.claim_sm_budget();
  work = selector.select_high_priority(decode, prefill_chunks);
  launch_kernels(work);
  stream_kv_appends();
  maybe_speculate();
}
"
